  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "The number of distinct pairs \\((x,y)\\) of real numbers satisfying both of the following equations: \\[ \\begin{aligned} x&=x^2+y^2,\\\\ y&=2xy. \\end{aligned} \\] is",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(4\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": "4"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1978-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "If \\(y=0,\\) the first equation gives \\(x=x^2,\\) so \\((x,y)=(0,0)\\) or \\((1,0).\\) If \\(y\\ne0,\\) the second equation gives \\(x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\) The first then gives \\(y^2=\\frac{1}{4},\\) producing \\((\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\) and \\((\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).\\) There are four pairs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Factor the second equation as \\(y(1-2x)=0\\)",
        "Handle \\(y=0\\) and \\(x=\\frac12\\) as separate cases"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
